-- title:
--   j-generator of the level-N function field is the q-expansion j(q)
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field and let $N$ be a positive natural number. Inside the field $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,K$ of formal Laurent series over $K$, let $K(j,j_N)$ denote the intermediate field [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldC K N`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L61), namely the $K$-subfield generated by the two elements `jqModC K` and `jqNModC K N`. Here `jqModC K` is the Laurent series $q^{-1}\cdot\varphi(\mathtt{jNum})$, where $\mathtt{jNum} = \mathtt{eisenstein4}^3\cdot\mathtt{dedekindEtaUnitInv}$ is a power series with integer coefficients and $\varphi$ is coefficientwise reduction along the canonical ring map $\mathbb{Z}\to K$; thus `jqModC K` is the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$ over $K$, with its simple pole at $q=0$ realised by the Hahn-series monomial $q^{-1}$. The theorem asserts the equality, as elements of the intermediate field $K(j,j_N)$, of [`ModularCurve.jGeomGen K N`](def/ModularCurve_CharLSpecialFibreLevelNDictionary.html#L82) with the pair consisting of the Laurent series `jqModC K` together with the membership proof [`ModularCurve.jqModC_mem K N`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L65) witnessing that this series lies in $K(j,j_N)$. No further hypotheses are imposed.
--
--   This identifies the distinguished element $\bar\jmath$ of the level-$N$ modular function field, as used in the description of the special fibre and of places of the modular curve, with the explicit subtype element built from the $q$-expansion of $j$. It serves as a bridge between the two spellings of $\bar\jmath$ occurring in the treatment of weight divisors and of the characteristic-three special fibre, and is cited in the results on the degree and on Riemann–Roch spaces attached to mod $p$ forms.
